    "data": {
      "A": "Develop a correctness-first high-throughput sequential implementation: replace quadratic per-bucket insertion sorting with a stable byte-wise LSD radix sort over the fixed-width strings, and use buffered/fast input and output. Preserve ordinary lexicographic ordering for every valid input, including duplicate keys; do not rely on benchmark-specific distributions. Measure against the 0.4225 s baseline.",
      "B": "Investigate a robust comparison-sort alternative: improve parsing/output buffering and sort record pointers/indices with an efficient library or custom comparator sort, optionally retaining independent first-byte buckets only when capacity and ordering are provably safe. Focus on variable/edge string content and exact output correctness rather than assumptions about uniform buckets.",
      "C": "Investigate a 4-core parallel design. Partition work without data races, use per-thread histograms/scatter regions or independently sortable ranges, and retain deterministic lexicographic output. Compare OpenMP/parallel radix or parallel sort approaches, accounting for setup and I/O overhead; all valid inputs must remain supported.",
      "G": "Integrate and benchmark the strongest complete candidate programs from A/B/C on the supplied correctness suite and timing protocol. Do not assume combined optimizations add speedups: retain only the fastest measured correct whole program. Recheck memory bounds, duplicate handling, declared string-width semantics, buffered final output, and portability on ARM64/4 CPU cores.",
      "analysis": "The baseline is correct but spends 0.4225 s using per-bucket insertion sort, which has quadratic worst-case cost and repeatedly invokes strcmp. The source has fixed allocated record stride (declared length plus NUL), so radix sorting is the highest-value route if fixed-width input semantics are honored. Input scanning and one-printf-per-record output are also likely material. Diverse sequential, comparator-based, and parallel research paths reduce risk; integration must select by measurements rather than merge them blindly."
